EXPERIMENT NO. 5: CIRCULAR MOTION OF A ROBOT USING TWO MOTORS

1. AIM

To write a Java program to create a robot that moves in a circular path using two motors with different speeds.

2. EXECUTION CODE

File Name: circlemotor.java

import ch.aplu.robotsim.*;

class circlemotor
{
    static
    {
        RobotContext.showNavigationBar();
    }

    circlemotor()
    {
        LegoRobot robot = new LegoRobot();

        Motor motA = new Motor(MotorPort.A);
        Motor motB = new Motor(MotorPort.B);

        robot.addPart(motA);
        robot.addPart(motB);

        motA.setSpeed(60);
        motB.setSpeed(30);

        motA.forward();
        motB.forward();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        new circlemotor();
    }
}

3. THEORY

Circular motion in robotics can be achieved by controlling the speeds of two motors independently. A motor converts electrical energy into mechanical motion and rotates the wheel connected to it.

In this experiment, a LegoRobot is created using the RobotSim library. Two Motor objects, motA and motB, are connected to MotorPort.A and MotorPort.B, respectively. Both motors are attached to the robot using the addPart() method.

The setSpeed() method sets the speed of each motor. Motor A is assigned a speed of 60, while Motor B is assigned a speed of 30. Both motors are then started using the forward() method.

When both motors rotate forward at equal speeds, the robot moves approximately in a straight line. However, when the motors rotate at different speeds, the robot continuously changes its direction and follows a curved path. The difference between the two motor speeds determines the curvature of the path.

In this program, Motor A rotates faster than Motor B, causing the robot to turn continuously and produce circular motion. The RobotContext.showNavigationBar() method displays the navigation bar in the simulation environment.

4. CONCLUSION

The practical demonstrates how to achieve circular motion using two motors in a simulated LEGO robot. By setting different speeds for Motor A and Motor B, the robot moves along a curved path. This experiment helps in understanding differential-drive motion and the role of independent motor speed control in robot navigation.
